{"question": "A 25-year-old zookeeper presents to the office complaining of a dry cough, fever, and chills for the past month. He states that the symptoms come in episodes at the end of the workday and last a few hours. He also mentions that he is fatigued all the time. His job includes taking care of various types of birds. He is otherwise fine and denies recent travel or trauma. Medical history is unremarkable and he does not take any medications. He does not smoke cigarettes or drinks alcohol. Allergies include peanuts, dust, and pollen. Childhood asthma runs in the family. Chest X-ray reveals diffuse haziness in both lower lung fields. A PPD skin test is negative. What is the most appropriate treatment for this patient?", "options": {"A": "Thoracocentesis", "B": "Inhaled beclomethasone", "C": "Avoid exposure to birds", "D": "Isoniazid for 6 months", "E": "Reassurance"}, "pred_answer": "C", "gold_answer": "C", "question_domains": ["Pulmonology", "Infectious Disease", "Occupational Medicine", "Allergy and Immunology", "Internal Medicine"], "option_domains": ["Pulmonology", "Occupational Medicine"], "question_analyses": {"Pulmonology": "Based on the symptoms and occupational exposure described in the scenario, the patient is likely suffering from a form of hypersensitivity pneumonitis, also known as bird fancier's lung, which is an allergic reaction to inhaled organic dust, particularly from birds. The key points leading to this diagnosis include:\n\n1. **Occupational Exposure**: The patient works as a zookeeper and is involved with various types of birds, which can expose him to bird droppings, feathers, and other organic materials that can trigger an immune response. 2. **Symptoms**: The dry cough, fever, chills, and fatigue that occur in episodes at the end of the workday suggest an acute reaction to inhaled antigens. The episodic nature of the symptoms aligns with hypersensitivity pneumonitis, which can present with acute, subacute, or chronic forms depending o