{"question": "A 33-year-old nurse is referred to an infectious disease specialist after she exhibited a PPD skin test with 17 mm of induration. She denies any cough, shortness of breath, hemoptysis, weight loss, fatigue, fevers, or night sweats over the last several months. Her temperature is 97.0\u00b0F (36.1\u00b0C), blood pressure is 120/81 mmHg, pulse is 82/min, respirations are 15/min, and oxygen saturation is 98% on room air. An initial chest radiograph is unremarkable. Which of the following is the most appropriate management of this patient?", "options": {"A": "Isoniazid", "B": "No management indicated", "C": "Quantiferon gold", "D": "Repeat PPD in 1 week", "E": "Rifampin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol"}, "pred_answer": "A", "gold_answer": "A", "question_domains": ["Infectious Disease", "Pulmonology", "Public Health", "Occupational Medicine", "Preventive Medicine"], "option_domains": ["Infectious Disease", "Pulmonology"], "question_analyses": {"Infectious Disease": "In this scenario, the 33-year-old nurse has a positive PPD (purified protein derivative) skin test with 17 mm of induration, which indicates a significant reaction consistent with latent tuberculosis infection (LTBI). The absence of respiratory symptoms (cough, shortness of breath, hemoptysis), systemic symptoms (weight loss, fatigue, fevers, night sweats), and an unremarkable chest radiograph suggest that she does not have active tuberculosis (TB) disease at this time. ### Key Points of the Scenario:\n1. **Positive PPD Test**: A 17 mm induration is considered positive in high-risk individuals, such as healthcare workers, indicating possible exposure to Mycobacterium tuberculosis. 2. **Asymptomatic**: The patient does not exhibit any symptoms typically associated with active TB, which is reassuring. 3. **Unremarkable Chest Radiograph**: This further supports the likelihood of LTBI rather than active TB disease. ### Management Considerations:\nThe most appropriate management for a patien